prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages pages in an internal company wiki.

The people involved are contributors, reviewers, space admins, and readers. One person may hold both the contributor and reviewer role at the same time.

The resources are pages and spaces. Every page records its author, reviewer, space, classification, whether external sharing is enabled, labels, and status.

Each requester carries their clearance and space memberships.

Each request carries whether it is on the corporate network, the current time, and whether emergency access is being used.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin may view a page only when their clearance is high enough for the page's classification.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin who authored a page may view their own page even when their clearance is not high enough for the page's classification.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin may view a page only when the page is not archived.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin must not view an archived page.

A request by a reader to view a page that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["Contributor", "Reviewer", "SpaceAdmin", "Reader"];

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    clearance: Long,
    spaceMemberships: Set<Space>,
};

entity Space;

entity Page {
    author: User,
    reviewer: User,
    space: Space,
    classification: Long,
    externalSharingEnabled: Bool,
    labels: Set<String>,
    status: String,
};

action viewPage appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Page],
    context: {
        isOnCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        currentTime: datetime,
        emergencyAccess: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
